./.planning/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_logbook.md:3:## 2026-08-19 — Build-host VPN safety boundary
./.planning/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_logbook.md:7:  so it was not reopened; the runner itself is now guarded before physical
./.planning/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_logbook.md:12:- Nineteen active macOS network-mutating physical validation tasks across
./.planning/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_logbook.md:15:  save, activate, start, route, or change DNS through a system VPN.
./LOGBOOK.md:24:- GATES: Every M0 viability row maps evidence for both candidates. libssh2 has no waived red row; ReluxNIOSSH's missing neutral TCP ownership, opaque signer, bounded intake, algorithm reporting, keepalive, and unbuilt adapter rows remain explicit. The four deferred semantics remain unsupported/notReported under `TASK-260728-3cveay`; physical scale, extension memory, soak, and DNS footprint remain M3 gates.
./LOGBOOK.md:25:- MEASUREMENT: Five consecutive current-tree, already-built real-OpenSSH M0 lifecycle runs exit 0 in 11.058–11.144 s test time. Whole-command maximum RSS is 71,254,016 B and maximum macOS peak footprint is 17,891,952 B; every lifecycle iteration asserts zero channels, socket, session, automatic tasks, custom allocations, and buffered bytes. This is a controlled harness envelope containing direct-tcpip, not an isolated DNS-open p99 or physical extension budget.
./LOGBOOK.md:29:- MAINTENANCE: BSD-3-Clause and Apache-2.0 notices remain mandatory; archive, patch, header, artifact, extension-safety, and negative source gates fail closed. `TASK-260728-3cveay` owns future selected-engine fork/rebase work. Any mandatory red, security advisory, patch-bound breach, platform/server-policy change, or red M3 physical result reopens ADR-014 rather than weakening a gate.
./LOGBOOK.md:46:- VERDICT: Changes requested and routed to development; format lint, core-boundary validation, and diff checks remain clean.
./LOGBOOK.md:120:- OWNER DECISION: `owner-approved-m0-vs-release-gate-decoupling.md` makes accepted physical-Mac smoke `TASK-260715-9yp8to` the technical prerequisite and keeps the blocked Apple account evidence APC34W-B1–B3 as a release-only gate. No release-readiness pass is inferred.
./LOGBOOK.md:146:- DECISION: The 4096-byte bound is checked over the actual binary-property-list representation of the exact three-key `providerConfiguration`, so owner and manager-contract metadata count toward the NetworkExtension envelope. The macOS adapter reads only `NETunnelProviderProtocol.providerConfiguration`; it has no App Group, Keychain, route, network, packet, or profile-write path.
./LOGBOOK.md:151:### 1616 — macOS-only Gate P0 passes on the physical Mac (TASK-260715-2ayxqn)
./LOGBOOK.md:153:- GATE DISPOSITION (supersedes the 2026-08-11 blocked reading): Overall macOS-only Gate P0 is PASS. Accepted C1 authority for the Relux Works r12 matrix, exact server-issued device-bound profiles, signed archive inspection, system approval, and physical provider lifecycle directly close every in-scope technical provisioning row. The `TASK-260715-apc34w` membership/agreement/Admin-field gaps remain release/account-assurance obligations and are not inferred closed, but the owner-approved active prototype scope does not treat them as unresolved P0 portal approval.
./LOGBOOK.md:155:- REVALIDATION: The four Mac Development profiles expire on 2027-08-10. Any profile or agreement/membership change affecting portal use, device or OS change, signing/capability/entitlement edit, system-approval reset, or identifier/archive drift requires the affected readiness, provisioning, archive, and physical lifecycle gates to be rerun. Agreement effective/expiry dates remain uncaptured and must be recorded before any release gate that depends on them.
./LOGBOOK.md:157:### 1549 — App Sandbox restores PlugInKit discovery and physical Gate P0 passes (TASK-260715-9yp8to)
./LOGBOOK.md:160:- ANOMALY/FIX: The first successful ten-cycle lifecycle attempt ended with runner exit 1 because `scutil --nc list` identifies the manager as `[VPN:works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac]`, not by the provider identifier. The manager-count parser now uses that exact host configuration ID and has a provider-decoy regression test; the clean rerun exits 0. Four contract tests pass with 91.80% coverage for `ProbeContract.swift`.
./LOGBOOK.md:163:### 0454 — Physical Gate P0 reaches the macOS VPN approval boundary (TASK-260715-9yp8to)
./LOGBOOK.md:164:- PASS: On the designated arm64 Mac15,9 running macOS 26.5/Xcode 26.5, the accepted archive passed all 47 nested signature, designated-requirement, profile, entitlement, architecture, and privacy assertions. Four contract tests pass with 91.80% line coverage for `ProbeContract.swift`; the new physical-runner parser/privacy tests and lint gates pass.
./LOGBOOK.md:165:- BLOCKER: The first lifecycle attempt waited the bounded 300 seconds without the required VPN-configuration approval being completed. It exited 1 before a manager or provider existed; post-attempt manager and provider-process counts are both zero, so the attempt left no stale state.
./LOGBOOK.md:166:- RESUME: An authorized person must accept the macOS VPN-configuration approval in the foreground probe window. Then rerun ten cycles, exact-path uninstall/reinstall, and the final cycle using the task-scoped redacted runbook. No Gate P0 pass is claimed before those physical checks succeed.
./LOGBOOK.md:185:- DEVICE POLICY: The locally available Mac is recorded only by a truncated SHA-256 reference, model class, OS, and architecture; no full device identifier is persisted. The physical-iPhone row remains the named ADR-024 deferred gap owned by `TASK-260715-1kntdx`, neither pass nor failure and not a macOS Gate P0 blocker.
./LOGBOOK.md:331:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
./LOGBOOK.md:334:- FINDING: installed products settle it in **neither** direction. Re-read on this Mac: Surfshark ships the `/Library/Keychains/` exception at `LSMinimumSystemVersion` **10.15**, bundled with `/private/var/db/mds/` and a third path; PureVPN's two providers carry `keychain-access-groups` at **10.15**; Tailscale's carries neither at **12.4**. Support ranges that wide isolate nothing about macOS 15.0. K1's clause runs **both ways** — a key present in a shipping bundle is not proof the mechanism functions, and a key absent from one is not proof it is unnecessary.
./LOGBOOK.md:399:- ROOT CAUSE (F2, and the more dangerous of the two): r5 corrected `TASK-260728-q5kjta` — the **human ceremony** — and left `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, the **unattended task that spends that authorization**, on the pre-r5 contract. Correcting an authorization node does not correct the node that consumes it. Its AC2 required the packet-tunnel entitlement "**only on provider identifiers**", which contradicted **r1**, not just r5: Apple requires `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has enforced it on all eight target rows since r2. Provider-only verification would have *passed* a portal state in which neither macOS host App ID carries the capability — surfacing at first build, after the sitting was over.
./LOGBOOK.md:424:- SCOPE: `PlatformVPNIdentity` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:17`) declares `appGroupIdentifier` as a non-optional `String` for both platforms — after G4 there is no macOS value to supply. Recorded for `TASK-260715-1tzaed`, not fixed; `production(for:)` still throws so nothing is broken today. `.spec/architecture.md:111` joins `security-claims.md` and threat-model M-02 on the spec-amendment list.
./LOGBOOK.md:429:- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
./LOGBOOK.md:432:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
./LOGBOOK.md:438:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
./LOGBOOK.md:440:- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
./LOGBOOK.md:445:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
./LOGBOOK.md:457:- FINDING: Verified against three shipping Developer-ID packet-tunnel products installed on this Mac — Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark. All three carry the suffixed value on **both** the containing app and the embedded sysex, confirming the Developer ID half. No development-signed counterpart exists locally, so the development half rests on the DTS statement and is re-checked by assertion A3 at first signed build.
./LOGBOOK.md:461:- FINDING: Read `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` off three shipping Developer-ID host/provider pairs on this Mac: hosts `false` in all three (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark), providers `true` in all three. Apple requires the **provider system extension** to be sandboxed; the containing app is not covered by that rule for direct distribution. r1 attributed both to a single Apple requirement.
./LOGBOOK.md:469:- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
./LOGBOOK.md:486:- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
./LOGBOOK.md:517:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 split. C1 is permission grants only (Keychain unlock, portal auth, identifier/profile authorization, notarytool profile, Sparkle keygen). The probe build `1r0fxv` and its independent review sit **outside** it, and Approval A1 (one click on the probe's system-VPN dialog) is a separate later interaction. The previous version claimed a human could remain present through a producer→reviewer cycle.
./LOGBOOK.md:521:- NOTE: `TASK-260715-39xz9g` requires real relux test-host access per its own scope. Nothing in the owner decision record confirms the agent has it. Flagged as conditional hold X1 rather than assumed autonomous.
./LOGBOOK.md:526:- DECISION: Owner-approved strategy encoded in `.spec/decisions.md` as ADR-023 (M0 SSH viability scope, Option A, with the four deferred semantics named and owned by TASK-260728-3cveay) and ADR-024 (macOS-only physical target, iOS deferred, Linux CI nonblocking, Relux Works signing/notary available). ADR-013 re-scoped from a blocking prototype gate to a release-path gate; ADR-014 moved Open → Accepted with libssh2 as the primary engine.
./LOGBOOK.md:529:- FINDING: The macOS path was silently gated on iOS through five non-obvious edges — `1idq8c`, `1lmmri`, `1uxx3i`, and `sbrrp7` all waited on `33oofa` (iOS targets), and `3lab1f` waited on `2hiabd` (iOS provider adapter). Those four alone blocked every macOS UI task, the macOS release archive, and all macOS physical provider validation. Deferring a platform is not just skipping its tasks; the cross-platform "shared" tasks have to be re-rooted too.
./LOGBOOK.md:530:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 deliberately includes the Gate P0 system-VPN approval click. The disposable probe builds in minutes from profiles created minutes earlier, so it stays one sitting. Hold H2 (real app, different bundle identifier) cannot be folded in and remains the next human boundary after 202 autonomous tasks.
./LOGBOOK.md:532:- VERIFICATION: `task-board validate` → valid, 0 issues (was 46). Independent cycle traversal → 0. No source tree change: `git status --porcelain -- Sources Tests Protocol relay scripts config Package.swift Makefile` is empty. Invariant scan confirms host-key-before-auth, Keychain-only secrets, fail-closed DNS, bounded memory, public packetFlow bridge, rootless exec/stdio relay, and signed/notarized release all survive the edit. Evidence: `.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/`.
./LOGBOOK.md:534:### 0421 — Security-testing review now routes to the provider that previously filter-failed on it (TASK-260728-1glezz)
./LOGBOOK.md:535:- FINDING: The new serial policy makes review Codex-owned and marks security, networking, DNS, parsers, signing, and release tasks `review=required`. The superseded `docs/spawn-policy.md` section it replaced recorded that Codex `gpt-5.6-sol` trips OpenAI's cybersecurity content filter on legitimate defensive security-testing work (fuzzing, hostile-input corpora, DNS/route leak tests, redaction tests) and exits non-zero mid-run.
./LOGBOOK.md:536:- ANOMALY: Removing that section was required by the task AC, so the highest-risk review categories are now routed to the provider with the known failure mode. Whether the filter still reproduces on `gpt-5.6-sol` is unverified — no security-testing review has run under the new policy yet.
